mber  Theory give examples of singular  integrals Calderon zigmund what theorems  do you know about them and how do you  prove boundedness I stated a TBE theorem  which nobody seemed very acquainted with  and said some brief explanation that  knowing boundedness on L2 was sufficient  to prove it for LP for a p between 1 and  infinity harmonic analysis is the  representation of functions as waves and  includes Fourier transforms the  questions that Terence was asked right  off the bat are Advanced and I don't  understand the answers but that's  because they're on very specific topics  you could make it all the way through a  math undergrad and PhD without ever  encountering Calderon Sigmund results of  singular integrals but these topics are  what Terence has told the examiners he  has prepared for and so that's where  they are guiding the questions since my  TB theorem was Martingale based Stein  asked me to give a definition of  martingales I gave the sigma algebra  purely measure theoretic version but  stumbled a bit on the definition of  conditional expectation I think Stein  was Satisfied by my explanation at the  end though Stein then asked me what  other boundedness theorems I knew I  couldn't think of any concrete ones  offhand but I mumbled about a  convolution operator being bounded if  it's Fourier transform was bounded and  had some smoothness condition but I  couldn't recall any details  it's here that we see things starting to  unravel for Terence at each step going  forwards he is being asked to  demonstrate a complex idea but chokes up  on the details and in providing concrete  examples Terence had studied harmonic  analysis for his master's thesis and  later said that since he felt it was his  strong suit he only spent a few days  reviewing analysis this TB theorem was  part of his master's thesis so he does  okay with questions about it but as soon  as they move away from it his lack of  preparation is starting to show there  were some questions about the  boundedness of the Hilbert operator and  an easy way of proof I said the Fourier  transform was bounded and that should do  it for L2 they seem to be hinting at  generalizations and eventually I clicked  onto Rhys potentials but again I didn't  know enough results only that they were  bounded with this being an oral exam we  see how different it is to open one  there are no set questions and the  examiners are coming up with questions  that respond to terence's answers this  allows them to really find the depth of  his knowledge in topics that he's  supposed to be prepared for the format  can be pretty intimidating at age 18  Terence would have been a few years  younger than his fellow students and had  found math easy ever since he was a  young kid with his natural interest and  talent for the subject he had never had  to try too hard studying for an exam in  the past and in earlier studies could  get by with cramming the textbook a few  days before the exam and spending the  rest of his time just playing with the  ideas that he found most interesting  according to an article he wrote years  later looking back at this exam he said  that he had made it all the way to  Princeton without developing systematic  study habits I was asked for a couple  definitions of H1 I got the atomic  definition right and the race potentials  that got the limit of harmonic functions  definition wrong and I had no idea how  to prove that any of them were  equivalent so they abandoned this after  a while but not until they made me  realize that the latter definition I had  given was actually that of L1 not H1  instead of treating the qualifying exam  as a top priority Terence said that he  spent an embarrassingly large fraction  of his time as a graduate student  messing around online which might be the  most relatable thing I've read about him  yet he discovered the World Wide Web in  his first year and played computer games  until late at night in the graduate  student dormitories computer room while  his fellow classmates did months of  preparation Terence said that he only  did about two weeks worth he might have  been used to getting away with doing  less work than those around him but  natural Talent can only get you so far  and they asked me about fundamental  Solutions I sluffed the exponents in the  denominator a few times so they began  asking me about homogeneity eventually  with a lot of prodding I got the  exponents of the Newtonian and kaushi  potentials correctly here the examiners  are asking easier and easier questions  to try and get him to a point where he's  able to give a satisfactory answer and  they're walking him through a derivation  of finding the fundamental Solutions of  the laplacian Terence later described  this as painful  to be clear for me none of this is easy  and barely any of the questions are even  comprehensible but I suppose it's  considered easy for someone who has  taken the right coursework kleinerman  asked me about the connections between  pdes and singular integrals but I didn't  know any more than vague generalities  Terence later said that he had enjoyed  playing with harmonic analysis for its  own sake but had never paid much  attention to how it was used in other  fields such as pdes this was the All Is  Lost moment but terence's luck was about  to change and they switched to analytic  number Theory surprisingly I feared much  better on this topic in a stroke of luck  only one of the three examiners had an  extensive background in number Theory  but had mistakenly thought that  terence's second special topic was  algebraic number Theory instead of  analytic number Theory and after  realizing his mistake he wasn't able to  use any of the difficult algebraic  number Theory questions which he had  prepared because that wasn't what  Terence needed to be tested on so  instead Terence was given just the  standard analytic number Theory  questions which weren't that difficult  Rudnick mainly asked give several forms  of the zeta function give one that  allows analytic continuation prove the  modularity of the Theta function and  thus derive the functional equation what  do you know about the poles and zeros  analytic number theory is using the  methods of analysis to study number  Theory the Riemann zeta function is  perhaps the most famous example where a  complex analysis is used to study the  distribution of prime numbers State a  form of the prime number theorem  throughout I didn't have to back up  anything with Messy calculations redneck  always stopped me when I tried  talk about primes and arithmetic  progressions give an elementary proof of  the infinitude of primes of the form 4N  minus 3. these were more standard  questions which Terence had prepared for  and so breezed through them the prime  number one is funny because Terence  later went on to prove quite a famous  theorem about prime number progressions  which are a series of numbers which are  equally spaced for example 3 7 and 11  constitute a progression of prime  numbers where the gap between each  number is four  Terence Tao and Ben green proved that it  is always possible to find somewhere in  the Infinity of integers a progression  of prime numbers of equal spacing and  any length what algebraic tools are used  in durashley's proof of infinitude of  primes and arithmetic progressions it  took a while before I understood oh you  want me to talk about characters then  they asked how duration got an explicit  formula for this when chai was a real  character I was going to write a messy  but finite expression involving signs  and logs but then realized that they  were talking about the class number  formula I said carelessly though that  this was a disgusting way to do it since  I was still thinking about the sine log  formulas then they made a comment that  this would put thousands of people out  of work or something like that anyway I  stated what the class number was vaguely  but didn't give out an explicit equation  again redneck wave me off when I tried  this and I seemed satisfied it seems  like here that Terence has accidentally  insulted anybody who uses the class  number theorem but when he said it was  disgusting it was because he was  thinking about writing out some other  messy expression a nice awkward  miscommunication but maybe they all  laughed about it then redneck asked a  few algebra questions only and it was  over talk about galwa Theory what do you  know about an what other simple groups  do you know the monster but I backed  down quickly saying I knew not how to  define it in the discussion meandered a  bit as the professors talked among  themselves about whether the group of  order 168 which I mentioned was sporadic  after this they decided to barely pass  me though they said that my harmonic  analysis was far from satisfactory they  didn't ask any real or complex analysis  but I guess from my handling of the  special topics they decided that wasn't  necessary besides we were almost getting  snowed in the exam lasted two hours  after the exam Stein who was his thesis  advisor told Terrence that he was  disappointed that his letters of  recommendation had been strong and this  wasn't what he was expecting  Terrence said that this moment left him  in a State of Shock because it was the  first time he had done poorly on an exam  that he had been genuinely interested in  doing well in it's not the first time he  had done poorly on an exam in fact he  had failed two of them back in undergrad  but one was for a Fortran class where he  had refused to learn it on the basis  that he already knew how to program in  basic and the other one was a quantum  mechanics class where Terence ignored  the warning that he would have to write  an essay about the history of the  subject during the final exam and he had  to be escorted from that final exam in  tears he said that the Princeton General  exam was a wake-up call and a turning  point in his career he began to take  studying and his classes more seriously  because he had finally seen consequences  for not studying I listened more to my  fellow students and other faculty and I  cut back on my gaming I worked  particularly hard on all the problems  that my advisor gave me in the hopes of  finally impressing him in retrospect  nearly failing the generals was probably  the best thing that could have happened  to me at the time being told that his  harmonic analysis was unsatisfactory is  kind of funny now because Terence Tower  was awarded the fields medal for his  contributions to partial differential  equations  combinatorics harmonic analysis and  additive number Theory either he did  become a lot better at harmonic analysis  or these exams are not great at  measuring one's ability to get a Fields  medal reading about Terence tau's  struggles with this exam is I'm sure  comforting to anyone who has struggled  with a test it's a reminder that tests  themselves don't say too much about your  potential but instead did what's more  important is what you do after getting a  bad result it also shows that really  dedicated and intelligent people can  sometimes mess up opportunities by  overestimating their own ability or  assuming they can Coast by purely on  natural Talent Terence tau is a top-tier  mathematician because of his creativity  in problem solving and in coming up with  new mathematics these aren't skills that  are being tested on a qualifying exam  but experience and being well read also  matter Terence himself says that one  does not need a genius Gene to become a  mathematician Talent is important of  course but how one develops and nurtures  it is even more so  this video is sponsored by male science  whose kits can help nurture curious  Young Minds  this is a cycloid drawing machine and  different combinations of the gears give  rise to different mesmerizing patterns a  gear with 60 teeth and a gear with 20  teeth have a ratio of three to one and  draw out a cycloid with three Loops for  every full spin of the large gear other  combinations will create different  shapes some of which we see in nature  drawing machine is from the art of math  kit it's from the Mel math subscription  which aims to change kids perceptions of  math and Inspire them with ideas that  they might not encounter at school the  math subscription is new and a part of  the Mel science line which includes  monthly boxes on stem physics chemistry  and medicine for kids from Age 5 to 16  plus the out of math kit also contains a  puzzle to explore the principles of  tessellation and Kevin Lieber from  vsauce2 provides guidance alongside the  projects through the mail science app to  keep the concepts accessible and fun I  think that these kits make for great  gifts for young people and have the  potential to inspire a lifelong interest  in science if you'd like to try them out  you can get 50 off your first month of  any mail Science subscription by using  my code tibies that link will be down in  the description thanks smell science and  thanks to my patreon supporters for  making these videos possible a special  shout out to today's patreon cat of the  day Hari Krishna  [Music]

